[2026.09.07-23.27.40:728][ 70]LogVoxelSky: VoxelSky SkyLight capture point now z=0.0 m (voxel.Sky.SkyLightAtGroundZ=0; the spawn column's ground is 1644.2 m, sea level is 0.0 m). THIS IS WHERE THE REAL-TIME CAPTURE IS RENDERED FROM (USkyLightComponent::SendRenderTransform_Concurrent -> FSkyLightSceneProxy::CapturePosition -> the cube view's ViewOrigin), so it is where the ambient term that lights every surface in this world is integrated -- and at night, with the moon down, it is the ONLY thing lighting them. At 0 the capture also sits far below the height fog's reference height, so it integrates fog rather than sky; voxel.Sky.FogInSkyCapture 0 is the other arm of that pair, and the fog's own APPLIED line prints the density at both altitudes.
[2026.09.07-23.27.40:728][ 70]LogVoxelSky: Sky: ExponentialHeightFog spawned. Ground ref z=1644.2 m, density=0.008, falloff=0.05, volumetric tier=1, r.VolumetricFog=1, voxel.Sky.Fog=1.
[2026.09.07-23.27.40:728][ 70]LogVoxelEarth: VoxelSky dome RESOLVED: Enabled=1 AtmosphereDome=1 (material=OK) RadiusUU=20000000 (200.0 km), source sphere radius 50.0 UU -> uniform scale 400000.0. Clipmap outer half-extent 65.5 km, corner 92.7 km; margin 2.16x. ONE radius serves both domes, deliberately -- it is a lower bound against that corner for each of them.
[2026.09.07-23.27.40:728][ 70]LogVoxelEarth: VoxelSky dome SHOWN (voxel.Sky.DomeEnabled=1, voxel.Sky.Enabled=1). This is the mesh only -- the light rig, the atmosphere and the exposure curve are unaffected either way, which is what makes DomeEnabled a clean A/B for 'how much of the night frame is the star dome'.
[2026.09.07-23.27.40:728][ 70]LogVoxelEarth: VoxelSky ATMOSPHERE DOME SHOWN (voxel.Sky.AtmosphereDome=1, materialValid=1 -> resolved 1). SHOWN means THIS MESH paints the sky and the SkyAtmosphere's full-screen pass does not (View.bSceneHasSkyMaterial goes true, SkyAtmosphereRendering.cpp:2214); HIDDEN hands the sky back to that pass. The two must agree to within 2/255 of mean luma at every hour -- that is the whole of phase S1, and this line is what tells a capture which arm it is.
[2026.09.07-23.27.40:728][ 70]LogVoxelSky: Sky rig spawned (deferred past the front end); the world session has started.
[2026.09.07-23.27.40:728][ 70]LogVoxelSky: Sky: height fog APPLIED. Read back off the component: visible=1, density=0.008, falloff=0.05, volumetric=1, inRealTimeSkyCapture=1, reference z=1644.2 m. Requested voxel.Sky.FogDensity=0.008 (times 1.000 for underwater suppression), voxel.Sky.FogHeightFalloff=0.05. EFFECTIVE density at the camera (z=1652.9 m) is 0.00776152; at the SkyLight's capture point (z=0.0 m) it is 2.38716. Those last two are what the frame and the ambient term actually see, and they are what to read if a fog cvar 'did nothing' -- if this line moved, the cvar applied and the answer is somewhere else.
[2026.09.07-23.27.40:728][ 70]LogVoxelSky: Sky: volumetric fog tier -1 -> 1 (GridPixelSize 16, GridSizeZ 64 -- conservative).
[2026.09.07-23.27.40:728][ 70]LogVoxelSky: VoxelSky material params bound to /Game/Voxel/MPC_VoxelSky.MPC_VoxelSky (27 scalars, 5 vectors in the asset). Driven EVERY FRAME, outside the voxel.Sky.ShadowUpdateHz gate.
[2026.09.07-23.27.40:728][ 70]LogVoxelSky: VoxelSky light ramp PUBLISHING to the marcher (Phase L2). sun/ambient tints by elevation: horizon(0) sun=1.25/0.72/0.42 amb=0.78/0.86/1.10 | noon(+60) sun=1.00/1.00/1.00 amb=1.00/1.04/1.12 | civil(-6) sun=0.70/0.72/0.95 amb=0.62/0.76/1.06 | night(-18, new moon) amb=0.55/0.68/1.00, full moon amb=0.80/0.91/1.18 | THIS FRAME alt=38.50 deg moonFrac=0.00000 sun=1.03/1.01/0.97 amb=0.98/1.03/1.12. Consumed only while voxel.March.VSLighting is 1; the noon row IS the marcher's shipped 1.00/1.04/1.12 ambient, so a noon frame is unchanged.
[2026.09.07-23.27.40:728][ 70]LogVoxelSky: VoxelSky moon DISC RESOLVED: 2.340 deg radius (4.68 deg across, 9.0x the real moon's 0.26), gain 0.150. At 2200x1300 / 90 deg FOV that is about 90 pixels wide. Written into /Game/Voxel/MPC_VoxelSky.MPC_VoxelSky (MoonAngularRadius + MoonBrightness), so the asset defaults are dead -- voxel.Sky.MoonAngularRadiusDeg and voxel.Sky.MoonDiscBrightness are the knobs. These move the DISC ONLY; the ground is lit by voxel.Sky.MoonIntensity.
[2026.09.07-23.27.40:728][ 70]LogVoxelSky: VoxelSky moon has SET: altitude -36.91 deg, azimuth 341.5 deg, 99% illuminated (phase 0.530, 0=new 0.5=full) at game 12.00 h with the SUN at +38.50 deg. MoonEnabled=1, disc radius 2.340 deg, voxel.Sky.MoonEpochOffsetDays=+0.000. A moon BELOW the horizon or at ~0% illuminated is not a bug -- both draw nothing on purpose. At offset 0 the shipped epoch puts this crossing at wall-clock +29.3 min into every session, identically, because the clock is solved to the same 37800.000 s every run; a NEGATIVE offset brings it forward.
[2026.09.07-23.27.40:728][ 70]LogVoxelSky: VoxelSky star map handedness RESOLVED: StarUDirection=+1.0 (read back from /Game/Voxel/MPC_VoxelSky.MPC_VoxelSky, raw +1.000), so StarRotation is being driven as +1.0 * (LST/360 + 0.0000 turns). Flipping the MPC scalar mirrors the map AND re-signs this drive together; driving +LST against a -1 map would rotate the sky backwards.
[2026.09.07-23.27.40:728][ 70]LogVoxelSky: VoxelSky StarAmbientGain RESOLVED to 1.0000, DERIVED from kStarAmbientCalibration (voxel.Sky.StarAmbientGain requested -1.0000; written into /Game/Voxel/MPC_VoxelSky.MPC_VoxelSky). Derived means appearance and illumination cannot drift apart unnoticed; the constant was measured at voxel.Sky.StarGain 0.15 against a snowfield whose ground/sky luminance ratio came out 0.790, i.e. snow's albedo. This is the SkyLight capture's star gain ONLY -- it cannot change a main-view pixel, because M_SkyAtmosphereDome routes it through a ReflectionCapturePassSwitch. It is the RATIO of capture star radiance to visible star radiance: voxel.Sky.StarGain (0.1500 here) is already inside the shared StarBrightness scalar both domes multiply, so this number carries none of it. 0 removes starlight from the ambient term entirely. Re-measure with tools/sky-albedo-check.py on a starlit capture: ground/sky luminance must equal the ground's albedo.
[2026.09.07-23.27.40:728][ 70]LogVoxelSky: VoxelSky moon colour RESOLVED: 5778 K illuminant (requested 12000 K, tint strength 0.00 against the sun's 5778 K), times the MEASURED lunar albedo (1.000, 0.975, 0.896) from T_MoonColor -- so the ground is lit by sunlight reflected off the same surface the disc is drawn with. Read back from the component: 5778 K. At tint strength 0 this is the SPECTRUM's answer, not a convention; voxel.Sky.MoonTintStrength 1 restores the old 12000 K blue and is the A/B -- see kMoonTemperatureK for why that argument is a real one.
[2026.09.07-23.27.40:728][ 70]LogVoxelSky: VoxelSky forward-shading PRIMARY is now the SUN (sun altitude +38.50 deg, moon altitude -36.91 deg, moon intensity 0.0000). Read back: sun priority 1, moon priority 0 -- these MUST differ or the editor's 'multiple directional lights are competing' warning returns (LightGridInjection.cpp:1662). This is the ONE light that lights translucency, water and volumetric fog; handover at sun -6.0 deg with 1 deg of hysteresis, derived from kMoonSunSuppressStartDeg.
